      "writtenOn": "2025-09-25T19:48:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Stupid ignorant question: does this mean that a pVM may be able to use more features than the host kernel allows it to use?",
      "revId": "b52e94c5e39766069f217c24c51a2804b9be6a91",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d0efc68d_73d18ddf",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/fpu/fpu.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 25,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-26T00:40:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, that is for simplicity as no need to have a separate channel to let the host kernel control what features can be used by pVM, and also didn\u0027t see the reason of doing that, so give the max features for the pVM. Please let me know if you saw any reason we may need to do that.",
      "parentUuid": "ce1183d8_3c9e5685",
      "revId": "b52e94c5e39766069f217c24c51a2804b9be6a91",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "170272b7_066831c3",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/fpu/fpu.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 25,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-26T09:45:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Although we ensure that the host has no access to pVM\u0027s own internal state, generally we treat pVM as less privileged than the host, not more privileged (at bit in line with what we discussed in https://android-review.googlesource.com/c/kernel/common/+/3762334/comment/a7f481bb_90fc9806/) and let the host manage pVM as usual.\n\nThat said, in this case, I\u0027m not familiar with this FPU stuff so I don\u0027t know what these permissions are about (i.e. for what reason the host needs to limit them for the guest), so I\u0027m not sure how important that is in this case...",
      "parentUuid": "d0efc68d_73d18ddf",
      "revId": "b52e94c5e39766069f217c24c51a2804b9be6a91",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "952195f9_371b48a4",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/fpu/fpu.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 25,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-26T10:19:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "According to the cover letter of the patch serial which introducd the fpu permissions: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20211021225527.10184-5-chang.seok.bae@intel.com/T/#m4ef0120b8b4060be2820ea593dc68dcf0cc1a658\n\n\"With AMX the FPU register state buffer which is part of task_struct::thread::fpu is not going to be extended unconditionally for all tasks on an AMX enabled system as that would waste minimum 8K per task.\".\n\nIts purpose is to save the overall memory on an AMX enabled system due to the large memory size (8k) required by the AMX FPU registers. Otherwise each process will consume additional 8k memory pages. But not from security perspective. So I think it is not causing the pVM being more privilege than the host. And for the pkvm hypervisor, enabling all features for pVMs doesn\u0027t cause significant memory consumption as we only have limited number of pVMs. So it is fine to give the max features to the pVMs.",
      "parentUuid": "170272b7_066831c3",
      "revId": "b52e94c5e39766069f217c24c51a2804b9be6a91",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "10a15731_6a8820d7",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/fpu/fpu.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 25,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-29T18:05:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see. I agree it shouldn\u0027t be a big problem for us to have extra 8K memory consumption per VM, or even per vCPU.\n\nOr perhaps it is even just per physical CPU, i.e. doesn\u0027t depend on the number of VMs?\n\nActually I\u0027d like to understand how and when all this memory is actually allocated:\n\n1. This per-pCPU `percpu_fpstate` is allocated statically, but it doesn\u0027t include the dynamically sized part of fpstate-\u003eregs, right? So, how and when do we allocate that dynamically sized part? Or we don\u0027t need to allocate it?\n\n2. Guest\u0027s fpstate is allocated in aosp/3728238 and possibly reallocated in aosp/3728240, but in both cases only before the pVM starts running, and in both cases it seems we assume we only need up to fpu_kernel_cfg.default_size, not fpu_kernel_cfg.max_size (donate_fpu() checks only for fpu_kernel_cfg.default_size)? BTW is that ok?\n\nSo when do we actually allocate up to fpu_kernel_cfg.max_size?",
      "parentUuid": "952195f9_371b48a4",
      "revId": "b52e94c5e39766069f217c24c51a2804b9be6a91",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5d35997e_4aeda1c4",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/fpu/fpu.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 25,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-30T01:49:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Or perhaps it is even just per physical CPU, i.e. doesn\u0027t depend on the number of VMs?\n\nWe have two kinds of fpstate. One kind is per physical CPU, and the other is for per vCPU.\n\n\u003e This per-pCPU percpu_fpstate is allocated statically, but it doesn\u0027t include the dynamically sized part of fpstate-\u003eregs, right? So, how and when do we allocate that dynamically sized part? Or we don\u0027t need to allocate it?\n\n`percpu_fpstate` is per physical CPU, and its purpose is to reset the FPU hardware with the initial FPU state before switching from pVM to host, so that the host cannot get pVM\u0027s FPU contents. So it is statically allocated, and doesn\u0027t include the dynamically sized part of fpstate-\u003eregs, as we don\u0027t need that to reset FPU hardware. The FPU hardware is reset by `restore_fpregs_from_fpstate(cur_fps, XFEATURE_MASK_FPSTATE)` in `fpu_swap_kvm_fpstate()`. cur_fps points to the percpu fpstate if this is guest exiting path. The restore will be executed according to the XSTATE_BV field in the XSAVE header, which is `fpstate-\u003eregs.xsave.header.xfeatures`. percpu fpstate has 0 xfeatures. According to the SDM (vol1 13.8 OPERATION OF XRSTOR and 13.12 OPERATION OF XRSTORS), the restore will update the field (indicated by XFEATURE_MASK_FPSTATE) to initial state if the field bit in XSTATE_BV is 0.\n\n\u003e (donate_fpu() checks only for fpu_kernel_cfg.default_size)? BTW is that ok?\n\nIt should be ok. The reason is that, the size is saved in `guest_fpu-\u003efpstate.size` although it is only checked against the default size, which may not be sufficient. But later when updating the CPUID via `pkvm_vcpu_after_set_cpuid()`, the `guest_fpu-\u003efpstate.size` will be checked properly against the desired size by `kvm_set_cpuid`-\u003e`kvm_check_cpuid`-\u003e`fpu_enable_guest_xfd_features`-\u003e`__xfd_enable_feature`(in pkvm/fpu/xstate.c). If the size is not sufficient, -ENOMEM will be returned. The `fpu-\u003eguest_perm` is used by `__xfd_enable_feature`, and it is pointed to `fpu-\u003eperm` by aosp/3728239.",
      "parentUuid": "10a15731_6a8820d7",
      "revId": "b52e94c5e39766069f217c24c51a2804b9be6a91",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
